---
// Egypt — Section 12: "What Still Lies Beneath?"
// Return to desert with massive negative space and open imagery.
import { desertOpenImage } from "../data/images"
---

<section
  class="relative overflow-hidden bg-background py-section"
  aria-labelledby="beneath-heading"
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-landing px-container-x sm:px-8 xl:px-14">
    <!-- Chapter marker -->
    <p class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">
      12 &mdash; What Still Lies Beneath
    </p>

    <h2
      id="beneath-heading"
      class="font-display text-headline-1 uppercase leading-none tracking-wider text-foreground"
    >
      We Have Not<br />Found Everything.
    </h2>

    <!-- Open desert image -->
    <figure class="mt-12 lg:mt-16">
      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-sm">
        <img
          src={`${desertOpenImage.src}&w=2000&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80`}
          srcset={`${desertOpenImage.src}&w=1200&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 1200w, ${desertOpenImage.src}&w=1600&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 1600w, ${desertOpenImage.src}&w=2000&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 2000w`}
          sizes="100vw"
          alt={desertOpenImage.alt}
          title={`Photo by ${desertOpenImage.author} on Unsplash`}
          width={desertOpenImage.width}
          height={desertOpenImage.height}
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="photo-tone w-full object-cover"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-muted">
        Photo by <a
          href={desertOpenImage.authorUrl}
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-muted underline hover:text-foreground"
          >{desertOpenImage.author}</a
        >
        on <a
          href={desertOpenImage.unsplashUrl}
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-muted underline hover:text-foreground">Unsplash</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-16 max-w-copy lg:mt-24">
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Modern archaeology has barely scratched the surface. Ground-penetrating
        radar has revealed previously unknown chambers inside the Great Pyramid.
        Satellite imaging has identified thousands of buried structures across
        the Nile Delta. Entire cities lie beneath the sand, unexcavated.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        New technologies — muon tomography, infrared thermography, LIDAR — are
        peeling back layers of earth and stone that have been undisturbed for
        millennia. Every season brings new discoveries. Every discovery rewrites
        what we thought we knew.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The Nile keeps flowing. The sand keeps shifting. And beneath it all,
        Egypt keeps its secrets — releasing them slowly, one excavation at a
        time.
      </p>

      <p
        class="mt-12 border-l-2 border-[var(--egypt-gold)]/40 pl-6 font-display text-headline-3 uppercase leading-snug tracking-wide text-foreground italic"
      >
        Five thousand years later, Egypt is still revealing itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
